// Soldier Palmer: the old British Red Palmer with a red tail, the classic bob fly of loch-style fishing.

id soldier-palmer
name "Soldier Palmer"
aliases "Red Palmer"
type wet
imitates attractor
naturals midge-pupa
difficulty intermediate
species trout grayling
season april may june july august september
origin region="Britain"
description "A short red wool tail, a red seal's fur body ribbed with gold wire, a red game hackle palmered its whole length and a longer collar in front."
history """
    One of the oldest flies on record. Walton (1653) has Barker's palmer flies, "some with red,
    and a red hackle", and Bowlker (1758) the Red Palmer of reddish mohair ribbed with gold
    twist under a red hackle: already the Soldier Palmer's materials. By 1892 Niven and Marbury
    print the name, Marbury saying the fly's red coat suggested it, and that a red body with the
    hackle at the head only is a Red Hackle. The red tail that tells it from
    the Red Palmer came later and is usual in Britain today; Marbury's American fly has none.
    Bodies are wool or seal's fur, hackles red game or ginger, one or two of them.
    """
fishing """
    The bob fly: on the top dropper of a team of wet flies on lochs and lakes, where the bushy
    palmer leaves a wake in the wave. It suggests nothing in particular, or a large red buzzer;
    it can be fished dry too.
    """
sources {
    "Project Gutenberg: The Compleat Angler (Izaak Walton)" url="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/683/pg683.txt"
    "Internet Archive: The Compleat Angler, facsimile of the 1653 first edition" url="https://archive.org/details/b24875454"
    "Internet Archive: The Art of Angling Improved (Richard Bowlker, 1758)" url="https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-art-of-angling-impro_bowlker-richard_1758"
    "Internet Archive: Favorite Flies and Their Histories (Mary Orvis Marbury, 1892)" url="https://archive.org/details/favoritefliesthe00marb"
    "Internet Archive: The British Angler's Lexicon (Niven, 1892)" url="https://archive.org/details/britishanglersle00niverich"
    "Ludlow Fly-Tying Club: Soldier Palmer (Derek Young)" url="https://ludlowflytying.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/pdf/Soldier-Palmer.pdf"
    "Trout & Salmon Fishing: Trout Flies (John Gray)" url="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/trout-flies.htm"
    "Sportfish: Soldier Palmer" url="https://www.sportfish.co.uk/hackled-wet-soldier-palmer.html"
    "Troutflies: Soldier Palmer" url="https://www.troutflies.co.uk/soldier-palmer-wet-fly"
    "Diptera: Soldier Palmer (archived)" url="https://web.archive.org/web/20251110154805/https://www.diptera.co.uk/patterns/s/soldier_palmer_ti.html"
    "Global FlyFisher: Soldier Palmer (Davie McPhail)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/soldier-palmer"
    "SoftHackles: Soldier Palmer" url="https://softhackles.blog/2020/01/10/soldier-palmer/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.sportfish.co.uk/media/catalog/product/2/1/216.1_11.jpg" page="https://www.sportfish.co.uk/hackled-wet-soldier-palmer.html" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view"
    "https://www.troutflies.co.uk/image/cache/wp/gj/%20ALL%20NEW%20PICS/BOX%20300-303/soldier%20palmer-550x550w.webp" page="https://www.troutflies.co.uk/soldier-palmer-wet-fly" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view, the gold rib clear"
}

hook wet-standard size=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tail wool color=red label="Red wool"
    rib gold-wire
    body seals-fur color=red label="Red seal's fur"
    // Sexyloops: palmered wet-fly hackles about 1.3 to 1.5 gapes.
    hackle cock-hackle color="#7d3b1c" fibre=1.1 sweep=35 label="Red game cock hackle"
    // Diptera: the collar "considerably longer" than the body hackle.
    collar hen-hackle color="#7d3b1c" fibre=1.4 label="Red game hen hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.88
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Ludlow: "slightly past the bend"; the photos show nearly a gape.
    tie-in tail length=1.1 spread=50 note="Short, on top."
    tie-in rib
    dub body to=0.84 note="Leave room for the hackle and the head."
    tie-in hackle note="By the stalk, at the front of the body."
    wind hackle to=0.05 turns=5 note="Two or three turns at the front, then open turns back to the tail."
    wind rib to=0.84 turns=5 note="Forward through the hackle, wiggled so it traps no fibres."
    tie-off rib
    trim hackle note="Cut the hackle tip away at the tail."
    tie-in collar
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind collar to=0.9 turns=2 note="Two or three turns, longer than the body hackle."
    tie-off collar
    whip-finish
}
